  PASS: the routeless container is removed, so the next acquire starts clean
  PASS: the primary network of a legacy container reads as the bridge
  PASS: acquiring against a legacy router succeeds
  PASS: by replacing it: the legacy container is removed and a new one created on the internal network
  PASS: and the operator is told why the router restarted
  PASS: first task takes a lease (this recreates the legacy router)
  PASS: a second task still gets a token
  PASS: but a legacy router with a live lease is not restarted under the task holding it
  PASS: the restart is deferred and announced instead
  PASS: reading the primary network of a missing container yields null rather than throwing into acquire

=== issue #2190: the token dies with the container ===
  PASS: the watcher waits on the task container itself, not on the solve process
  PASS: nothing is revoked while the container runs
  PASS: the token is released the moment the container exits
  PASS: for the session that owned it
  PASS: against the router that issued it
  PASS: and the exit status is recorded, killed or not
  PASS: a container docker cannot wait on is not running either, so its token is released too
  PASS: with the daemon message as the reason
  PASS: stopping the watcher ends its docker wait
  PASS: and a stopped watcher does not revoke: the caller took over the lease
  PASS: which is what it reports
  PASS: a docker wait that could not run says nothing about the container, so the maintenance tick keeps the job
  PASS: and the watcher reports that it did not release
  PASS: an external router issues no per-task token, so there is nothing to watch
  PASS: and without a container name there is nothing to wait on
[router-isolation] Could not start 'docker wait task-f', the token will be revoked by the next maintenance tick instead: docker missing
  PASS: a spawn that throws is reported and leaves the token to the maintenance tick

